    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.pairs\n\n### Description\n\nThe setup i'm trying to have is having `vim.g.minipairs_disable = true` by default, \r\nand then in each buffer toggle it on or off, either with a map or autocmd (`autocmd Filetype c let b:minipairs_disable=0`)\r\n\r\nIn my opinion it makes more sense to have this \"off by default\" setup, but it seems to clash with the way the global disable currently works.\r\nIf something is globally disabled, it doesn't matter if you turn it on in the buffer, it won't work until you enable it\r\nglobally, and that makes it \"on by default\"\r\nAny workaround? Anything I'm missing?",
